  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/ef/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_9.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Spider-Man rushes through town in such a hurry, he's paying attention to nothing else until he arrives home, and we learn that he was out filling Aunt May's prescription.&nbsp; Apparently her illness is serious.&nbsp; Meanwhile, a new character named Electro suits up and gives himself a full charge before going out and robbing an armored car filled with gold.\n\nThe next morning, the visiting doctor tells Peter that his Aunt May is worse and needs to go to the hospital.&nbsp; He ignores everyone in school before rushing to the hospital, where the doctor is preparing to operate, and he finds Betty Brant sitting with his aunt.&nbsp; He greets them before heading back out as Spider-Man - he has to raise money for the operator, and the only way to do that is to take pictures of Spider-Man and sell them to the Bugle.&nbsp; But rain spoils that plan (his camera isn't good enough to take photos in the rain), and Peter goes home, worried about money, Aunt May <i>and</i> his upcoming mid-term exams.\n\nNext day, Jonah Jameson is at his bank when Electro barges in!&nbsp; Electro recognizes Jameson, which the latter thinks is perhaps a clue that they're acquaintances.&nbsp; He robs the bank and then is able to scale the wall using electricity to lock onto it.&nbsp; Jameson jumps to the conclusion that Electro is actually Spider-Man, especially since Spider-Man <i>himself</i> swings by a moment later!&nbsp; The next edition of the Daily Bugle headlines with ELECTRO IS REALLY SPIDER-MAN!\n\nPeter is irritated about this, but he's busy at the hospital, where he learns that the specialist they've brought in to operate on May charges $1000!&nbsp; He tries to get Jameson to lend him the money, but he refuses.&nbsp; Spider-Man then scours the city, looking for some crime to use to photograph himself and eventually runs across Electro!&nbsp; Spider-Man tries to grab him but is shocked by Electro, and he crumples.&nbsp; Electro thinks he's dead (even to the point of thinking \"I didn't mean to do that\") and leaves.\n\nWhen Peter wakes up, he decides to take pictures of himself superimposed over Electro so that Jameson will think he snapped Electro changing into Spider-Man.&nbsp; He hates selling these photos to Jameson under false pretenses, but he desperately needs the money!&nbsp; Jameson prints the photos on the front page as proof that Spider-Man is Electro, which makes the latter laugh and think about his former self, an electrician named Max Dillon.&nbsp; But one day, while he was up on the pole, a million-to-one electric bolt gives him the power to control electricity, which he practices with before adopting the moniker Electro.\n\nNow he heads to the prison to free some inmates who can help fend off the police during his crime spree.&nbsp; But Aunt May calls for Peter just before her operation, and Peter promises to wait, with Betty showing up to keep him company.&nbsp; Meanwhile, Jameson is thrilled that Spider-Man isn't showing up at the prison to stop Electro because it proves that Electro <i>is</i> Spider-Man!&nbsp; Electro is able to convince some inmates to wait and follow his lead.\n\nAt the hospital, the operation is a success, and Peter greets his recovering aunt before heading out.&nbsp; Betty urges him to stay and not go after the dangerous photos, sensing that Peter is starting to enjoy the danger \"just like someone <i>else</i> I once knew.\"&nbsp; But Peter heads out, and soon Spider-Man is stopping by a store to pick up a few supplies before heading out to face Electro.&nbsp; When Spider-Man arrives to face Electro, Jameson realizes he's been tricked and buries his head.&nbsp; He'll be a laughing stock, \"and all because of Peter Parker!\"\n\nParker pauses long enough to put on the rubber gloves and galoshes to protect himself from Electro's body, and he proceeds to hurl metal objects at Electro, which deflect the oncoming bolts, but Electro knows he'll run out of things to throw soon.&nbsp; Electro swings electrified metal wires at him, but Spider-Man grabs a water main hose and douses Electro with it, short-circuiting him!&nbsp; After unmasking him and realizing he has no idea who Electro is, he heads toward the Bugle to give Jameson the new photos.\n\nJameson is livid and demands that \"blanketty-blank #$%#*\" be shown in, threatening to sue!&nbsp; But Peter stops him with genuine action shots of Spider-Man fighting Electro!&nbsp; Jameson changes his tune of course and offers to take these pictures in exchange for the fake one, to which Peter agrees.&nbsp; Jameson knows he's robbing him and that he'll make a fortune with these photos, but Parker deserves it because he's a fool!&nbsp; (And a <i>fantastic </i>panel showing his evil grin to close out page 21, I might add - <i>ha!</i>)\n\nPeter and Betty have a brief spat about how Betty was hurt before, but the two eventually walk off together.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "a solid enough book that encapsulates many of the things we come to see in Spider-Man tales over the upcoming years.&nbsp; This was the first time Aunt May has an illness that keeps Peter from chasing a criminal, and even though it gets <i>way</i> overused in the years to come, it's fresh and new here.&nbsp; The tale of Electro wasn't as fascinating as that of Doc Ock or even the Sandman, but he does at least join them in the rogues gallery, and the story flowed pretty well.&nbsp; In fact, this time it didn't even seem like there were quite enough pages to tell it as thoroughly as it might have deserved, which just leaves us wanting a bit more, and what else can you ask from an issue anyway - you <i>want</i> to make sure you don't miss one!&nbsp; So well done by Lee and Ditko!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt won't rise to the level of all-time Spider-Man greats, but it was a nice intro to a new villain nonetheless.\n\nAn interesting note in the letters pages is the question put forth by Gordon Flagg Jr from Atlanta, GA who asks why every sentence except questions ends with exclamation marks rather than periods, and the honest answer is stated that periods do not always reproduce well in the print process, making it look like there are run-on sentences.&nbsp; \"But exclamation marks show up much better, thus eliminating the possibility of any confusion.\"&nbsp; (This is raised again later by Gerald Boucher in the letters page of DD #6, with the explanation this time being:&nbsp; <i>As for the exclamation marks -!&nbsp; As you may notice -!&nbsp; We just plain <b>like</b> 'em!!</i>)\n\nThe letter pages seem split on whether Steve Ditko's art is any good, but most think his work on Spider-Man himself is adequately quirky.\nThe announcements page indicates that this issue #9 is having to be sent to the printers before #7 has even hit the stands, so they still don't know if they'll get the okay from the fans about dropping the letters page in <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> - I can't wait to see the avalanche of nay-sayers!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}